# Guides for scales: guides

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`guides()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/guides.html), in the order the page gives
them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet
keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what
remains.

Every scale is read back through a guide: an axis for a position scale, a legend or a colour bar for
the others. Each scale's `guide` argument sets its guide; `guides()` sets them all at once, by
aesthetic, each as a guide's name, such as `"legend"`, or a call to a guide function. A guide set to
`"none"` is left out, and legends that show the same values under the same title merge into one.
Giggly reads each scale back as ggplot2 does by default: `x` and `y` with axes, a continuous
`colour` or `fill` with a colour bar, and every other with a legend. It sets the guides of all but
the axes with `Giggly.Plot.guides/2` or a scale function's `guide:`, each by name, `:colorbar`,
`:legend` or `:none`, or as the legend `Giggly.Plot.guide_legend/1` builds, and merges legends that
read alike, as ggplot2 does; it has no `guide_colourbar()`, nor `guide_legend()`'s `title` option.
A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG
document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines
`render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`.

## The guides ggplot2 chooses

`p` maps `colour` to a continuous column and `size` and `shape` to discrete ones (`factor()` makes
a column discrete), so ggplot2 reads `colour` back with a colour bar and the others with legends.

```r
# ggplot object

dat <- data.frame(x = 1:5, y = 1:5, p = 1:5, q = factor(1:5),
 r = factor(1:5))
p <-
  ggplot(dat, aes(x, y, colour = p, size = q, shape = r)) +
  geom_point()

# without guide specification
p
```

Giggly's table holds `q` and `r` as text, `"1"` to `"5"`, a discrete column as R's factors are.

```elixir
dat = %{
  "x" => Enum.to_list(1..5),
  "y" => Enum.to_list(1..5),
  "p" => Enum.to_list(1..5),
  "q" => ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5"],
  "r" => ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5"]
}

p =
  dat
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :x, y: :y, colour: :p, size: :q, shape: :r})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

render.(p)
```

## Choosing each guide

```r
# Show colorbar guide for colour.
# All these examples below have a same effect.

p + guides(colour = "colorbar", size = "legend", shape = "legend")
p + guides(colour = guide_colorbar(), size = guide_legend(),
  shape = guide_legend())
p +
 scale_colour_continuous(guide = "colorbar") +
 scale_size_discrete(guide = "legend") +
 scale_shape(guide = "legend")
```

Giggly draws the first and the third, where `scale_colour_continuous()` is
`Giggly.Plot.scale_color_continuous/2` and `scale_shape()` is `Giggly.Plot.scale_shape_discrete/2`;
the second calls `guide_colorbar()`, ggplot2's other spelling of `guide_colourbar()`.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.guides(colour: :colorbar, size: :legend, shape: :legend)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.scale_color_continuous(guide: :colorbar)
|> Plot.scale_size_discrete(guide: :legend)
|> Plot.scale_shape_discrete(guide: :legend)
|> render.()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `guide_colourbar()`.

## Removing guides

```r
 # Remove some guides
 p + guides(colour = "none")
 p + guides(colour = "colorbar",size = "none")
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.guides(colour: :none)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.guides(colour: :colorbar, size: :none)
|> render.()
```

## Merged legends

Legends with the same title that show the same values merge into one, each key showing a colour,
a size and a shape together.

```r
# Guides are integrated where possible

p +
  guides(
    colour = guide_legend("title"),
    size = guide_legend("title"),
    shape = guide_legend("title")
 )
# same as
g <- guide_legend("title")
p + guides(colour = g, size = g, shape = g)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `guide_legend()`'s `title` option.

## Legends below the plot

```r
p + theme(legend.position = "bottom")
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.theme(legend_position: :bottom)
|> render.()
```

## The order of the legends

```r
# position of guides

# Set order for multiple guides
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, cty)) +
  geom_point(aes(size = hwy, colour = cyl, shape = drv)) +
  guides(
   colour = guide_colourbar(order = 1),
   shape = guide_legend(order = 2),
   size = guide_legend(order = 3)
 )
```

Not in Giggly yet: `guide_colourbar()`, and the `order` option of `guide_colourbar()` and
`guide_legend()`, which sets the order the guides are drawn in.